Out-of-tree build support for kernels older than the API the driver follows.

The driver in drivers/gpu/drm/fl2000/ uses the current DRM API, exactly as it
is submitted upstream. When the top-level Makefile finds that the target
kernel still uses older names, it copies the sources to .compat-build/ and
applies the matching sed rules below before building. Nothing here is part of
the upstream patches.

  atomic-commit.sed   struct drm_atomic_commit was struct drm_atomic_state
  create-state.sed    crtc/plane/connector .atomic_create_state was .reset
  bridge-state.sed    bridge .atomic_create_state was .atomic_reset

When a new kernel renames something the driver uses, update the driver to the
new name and add a rule here for the old one.

DRM helpers missing from the target kernel
------------------------------------------

The HDMI connector is drm_bridge_connector, which the Kconfig selects
(DRM_BRIDGE_CONNECTOR, with the HDMI state, audio and CEC helpers it calls).
Kernels for PCs are usually built without it, as only SoC drivers select it.
The top-level Makefile looks the helpers' symbols up in the target's
Module.symvers and compiles the missing ones into fl2000.ko, unchanged, from

  drm-helpers/<major.minor>/   drivers/gpu/drm/display/*.c of that kernel
                               series, with SHA256SUMS and SOURCE

For a series that has no directory yet, the Makefile runs
fetch-drm-helpers.sh, which downloads the files of the target's own release
from the stable tree (GitHub mirror). Commit the new directory: builds for that
series then work offline, and the Makefile checks the files against
SHA256SUMS. The helpers are not translated by the sed rules, since they come
from the target kernel itself. Idea taken from klogg/fl2000_drm, which builds
the same files for its distribution packages.
